  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Seoul Metro Line",
    "overview": "The whole site is a working wayfinding system. Station-enamel ground, signage-navy panels, and the real Seoul line colours used as a code: green is career and product work, orange is applied AI and \"now\", violet is the playground. Projects are stations with codes (A01, P04, X07), roles are stops on a single green line, and the contact section is the terminus. Every device is borrowed from the metro itself: circular roundels with a line-coloured ring, transfer double-rings, 10px line strokes, a three-colour stripe under the sign band, yellow exit-number squares, and a side-on train car that marks the selected station.\n\nDensity is that of a station platform sign: one huge name, short supporting text, generous section breathing room (128px vertical on desktop), and information grouped by line rather than by card grid. Light enamel carries most of the page; the navy sign colour appears as bands and panels (the sticky sign bar, the arrival screen, the terminus), never as a full dark theme. The only motion is travel: the train glides along the line with a long ease-out, and the line scrolls the selected station into view on phones. Nothing else animates.\n\nType is one family doing all the work. Archivo stretched to 112% width and set heavy reads as station-name signage; the same family at normal width carries body copy. Korean falls back to Gothic A1 Heavy for display and the platform Korean sans for body, with `word-break: keep-all`, so the trilingual sign stacking (Latin name over Hangul name) holds in EN, ES and KO.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Line colour is a code with meaning, never decoration.",
      "Roundels, line strokes and transfer rings are the recurring structural marks.",
      "Flat enamel and navy surfaces; depth comes from tonal panels, not shadow.",
      "One stretched heavy sans for display, same family for body.",
      "Motion is linear travel along the line only."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Line Code Rule",
        "body": "Green means product/career, orange means applied AI or \"now\", violet means playground. A line colour appears only where it states that membership; never pick one for looks.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hard Stop Rule",
        "body": "Where lines meet, colours change at a hard stop (stripe segments, per-station track segments). Lines are never blended into gradients.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Text-Safe Orange Rule",
        "body": "Orange as text on enamel uses Line 3 Ink; bright Line 3 Orange is for strokes, fills, underlines, and text set on navy or with ink on top of it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Station Sign Rule",
        "body": "Anything that names a place (person, project, line, company) is set in the stretched 112% width; anything that explains it is set at normal width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Trilingual Stack Rule",
        "body": "Names stack Latin over Hangul at reduced weight in steel; Korean text always uses `word-break: keep-all`.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 10px Line Rule",
        "body": "Every line stroke (featured track, stems, experience line, terminus stripe) is 10px thick with rounded or hard ends; the sign bar stripe is the one thinner (6px) echo.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Lit Screen Rule",
        "body": "Only the arrival screen casts a shadow. Everything else sits flat on the platform.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** encode meaning with line colour: green (#00a84d) product/career, orange (#ef7c1c) applied AI / now, violet (#996cac) playground.",
      "**Do** draw lines as 10px strokes with hard colour stops where lines change.",
